   Ada [a quarter past seven in the evening at Mrs. Turner's two rooms in Bermondsey] (as usual)
      → Read the evening paper that Bert brought home (1d.)
      ⇒ Ada read the evening paper: LONDON. German bombers striking at the vital heart of Britain’s Empire gouged hard at London today in the longest and most ferocious bombardment of the war — nine hours and 41 minutes of fire-streaked, bomb-blasting horror. The raid began at 7:58 p. m. Sunday, as night closed in, shrieked on with rising intensity through the… BERLIN. A German military source said that mass German air attacks on London would go on day after day and night after night until Great Britain’s resistance is smashed. The attacks of Saturday night and Sunday were by far the most severe thus… LONDON. The air raid sirens wailed again at 8:30 o’clock tonight (2:30 p.m., E. S. T.). By the Associated Press. LONDON, Sept. 9.—Nazi bombers returned to London late today, apparently for the third night of Adolf Hitler’s grimmest bombings, but an air alarm in the British capital lasted only 1 hour and 15 minutes. It was believed, however, that—as on previous nights— the first wave of attackers merely was a vanguard blazing a trail for swarms to come. They already have left approxi approximately - mately approximately 600 persons dead and 2,600 seriously wounded in two severe night-long raids.

